## 10. Interview questions and answers

**1. Why keep messages in a separate table from conversations?**  
Conversation metadata changes infrequently, while message history grows with each turn. Separate rows keep the conversation record small and make history retrieval, ordering, and deletion explicit.

**2. Why use a junction table for conversations and documents?**  
The relationship is many-to-many: one conversation may use several documents, and users may select a document in several conversations. The junction table stores those links and can enforce that both records belong to the same user.

**3. How does the system prevent one user from opening another user’s conversation?**  
Every conversation lookup includes both its ID and the authenticated user’s ID. The user ID comes from the verified JWT, not from the request body.

**4. What happens if assistant generation fails?**  
No messages have been written yet. The user and assistant messages are added in one database transaction only after generation succeeds, so failure leaves the conversation history unchanged.

**5. How are retrieval scopes enforced?**  
Single and selected scopes use the document IDs stored in `conversation_documents`; all-documents scope resolves the authenticated user’s completed documents. Retrieval receives both the user ID and the permitted document IDs.